    a: "<b>Driving and Dementia:</b><br><b>Key principle:</b> Dementia <b>eventually impairs driving ability</b> — but the threshold varies by severity and domain affected.<br><b>When to recommend driving cessation:</b><ul><li>Moderate-to-severe dementia (MMSE &lt;20) — generally unsafe</li><li>History of accidents, getting lost while driving, family concerns</li><li>Any dementia affecting visuospatial function, judgment, or reaction time</li></ul><b>Evaluation options:</b><ul><li>Neuropsychological testing (trail making test B most predictive)</li><li><b>On-road driving evaluation</b> by certified driving rehabilitation specialist — gold standard</li></ul><b>Legal/ethical considerations:</b><ul><li>Physician duty to warn varies by state (some states require mandatory reporting)</li><li>Document discussions thoroughly</li><li>Patient may lack insight — involve family/caregiver</li></ul><b>Alternatives:</b> Arrange transportation, volunteer driver programs, ride-sharing"
