    q: "What is the diagnostic approach to a patient with rapidly progressive dementia (RPD)?",
    a: "<b>Rapidly Progressive Dementia (RPD) — Approach:</b><br><b>Definition:</b> Dementia progressing to severe impairment within <b>weeks to months</b> (vs. years in typical AD)<br><b>Key causes to consider:</b><ol><li><b>Prion disease (CJD)</b> — most feared; rapid course, myoclonus</li><li><b>Autoimmune encephalitis</b> — anti-NMDAR, anti-LGI1, anti-CASPR2 (treatable!)</li><li><b>Infectious</b> — herpes encephalitis, HIV, TB, syphilis</li><li><b>Neoplastic/paraneoplastic</b> — limbic encephalitis, carcinomatous meningitis</li><li><b>Toxic-metabolic</b> — Wernicke's, drug toxicity, B12 deficiency</li><li><b>Vascular</b> — multiple strokes</li></ol><b>Workup:</b><ul><li>MRI with DWI (CJD: cortical ribboning)</li><li>EEG (CJD: periodic sharp waves; NCSE)</li><li>LP: 14-3-3, tau, RT-QuIC, autoimmune panel, cultures, cytology</li><li>Paraneoplastic panel (serum + CSF)</li><li>CT chest/abdomen/pelvis, PET scan (occult malignancy)</li></ul>"
