    a: "<b>Posterior Cortical Atrophy (PCA) — 'Visual Variant of AD':</b><br><b>Key feature:</b> Predominant <b>visuospatial and visuoperceptual deficits</b> rather than early memory loss<br><b>Clinical presentation:</b><ul><li>Difficulty reading, writing, recognizing faces (<b>prosopagnosia</b>)</li><li>Getting lost in familiar environments</li><li>Apraxia (difficulty performing learned movements)</li><li>Constructional difficulties</li><li>Symptoms of <b>Balint syndrome</b> (simultanagnosia, optic ataxia, ocular apraxia)</li><li>Memory relatively preserved early</li></ul><b>Pathology:</b> Usually Alzheimer's pathology (amyloid plaques + NFTs) concentrated in <b>posterior parieto-occipital cortex</b><br><b>Imaging:</b> MRI shows posterior cortical atrophy (parietal/occipital predominance)<br><b>Treatment:</b> Same as typical AD (ChEIs + memantine)<br><b>Epidemiology:</b> Typically younger onset (50s–60s)"
