    a: "<b>Sundowning vs. Delirium in Nursing Home Patients:</b><br><b>Sundowning:</b><ul><li>Exacerbation of dementia-related behavioral symptoms (confusion, agitation, wandering) in <b>late afternoon/evening</b></li><li>Not a separate diagnosis — it is a <b>pattern</b> of symptom timing</li><li>Caused by: Disrupted circadian rhythm, fatigue, reduced light, staffing changes</li><li>Underlying cognition is at <b>patient's baseline</b> dementia level (not acutely worse)</li></ul><b>Delirium:</b><ul><li><b>Acute onset</b> — different from patient's usual baseline</li><li>New or worsening medical cause identifiable</li><li>Inattention is hallmark</li></ul><b>Distinction matters because:</b><ul><li>Sundowning: Managed with behavioral/environmental approaches + circadian interventions</li><li>Delirium: Requires medical workup to identify and treat precipitant</li></ul><b>A new onset of 'sundowning-like' behavior in a patient without prior history = DELIRIUM until proven otherwise</b>"
